<PageLayout title='Genomic Architecture & Local Genealogies' kicker="RESEARCH" lead='A genome is physically continuous, but its ancestry does not have to be.'>
<section><div class="prose content-page"><p>Recombination means neighboring regions can trace their ancestry through different genealogical paths. Over time, selection, drift, introgression and linked processes can make some regions coalesce much deeper or much more recently than others.</p><p>This line asks whether those local histories form meaningful evolutionary blocks, how stable they are across chromosomes, and whether regions with similar coalescent histories share functional or structural properties.</p><h2>$ current_questions</h2><ul><li>Can genomic regions be classified into recurrent TMRCA regimes?</li><li>Are deep, intermediate and recent coalescent blocks randomly distributed?</li><li>Do local genealogical regimes correlate with genes, recombination or chromosomal organization?</li><li>When does a genomic block reflect shared ancestry rather than selection or technical artefact?</li></ul><div class="quote">The chromosome is one molecule. Its history can still be plural.</div></div></section>
</PageLayout>